WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.560
Cells release tiny sacs of membrane called extracellular vesicles.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.520
These vesicles can carry a signal from one cell to another.

3
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.520
The clearest case comes from the immune system.

4
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:20.760
B cells release vesicles that carry MHC class II molecules, each holding a peptide.

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.560
Those vesicles switch on T cells, but only the T cells whose receptor recognises

6
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.620
that peptide. Nothing has to get inside.

7
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.320
A scrap of membrane can show a T cell what a whole B cell would show it.

8
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:37.093
Where do these vesicles come from?

9
00:00:37.793 --> 00:00:39.453
The answer starts inside the cell.

10
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:45.093
The membrane of a compartment called an endosome buds inward, away from the cytosol,

11
00:00:45.493 --> 00:00:46.553
the cell's own fluid.

12
00:00:47.373 --> 00:00:51.673
It pinches off small vesicles into its own interior, and these carry receptors.

13
00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:55.713
A compartment packed with them is a multivesicular body.

14
00:00:56.653 --> 00:01:01.873
In B cells, compartments like this are where MHC class II is loaded with peptide.

15
00:01:01.873 --> 00:01:01.893
Thank you for watching.

16
00:01:01.893 --> 00:01:02.253
If you enjoyed this video, please like, share, and subscribe.

17
00:01:02.253 --> 00:01:02.867
Thank you for watching.

18
00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:07.547
Picture this compartment as a sorting room with two doors, – one to the incinerator

19
00:01:07.547 --> 00:01:08.627
and one to the street.

20
00:01:09.427 --> 00:01:13.687
If it fuses with a lysosome, its vesicles and their cargo are digested.

21
00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:19.267
If it moves to the cell surface and fuses with the plasma membrane, its vesicles are

22
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:23.067
released intact. Those released vesicles are exosomes.

23
00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:28.107
The route was first seen in young red blood cells, throwing out transferrin

24
00:01:28.107 --> 00:01:29.827
receptors they no longer needed.

25
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.227
The direction of budding fixes the geometry.

26
00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:39.147
The bud forms away from the cytosol, so the inside of an exosome is a sample of

27
00:01:39.147 --> 00:01:43.567
cytosol, and its outer face is the face that was the outside of the cell.

28
00:01:44.387 --> 00:01:49.587
So a receptor, or an MHC molecule, still points outward, as it did on the plasma

29
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:53.887
membrane. That is why a vesicle can show a ligand to another cell.

30
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.710
A vesicle could act on another cell in three ways.

31
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:03.010
A ligand on its surface can bind a receptor on the target, and nothing has to get

32
00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:08.430
in. The vesicle can fuse with a membrane of the target and release its contents into

33
00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:11.330
the cytosol. Or the target can swallow it.

34
00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:16.350
Swallowing alone shows little, because what a cell swallows usually ends in the

35
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.350
lysosome.

36
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:23.153
Proving a case takes controls, because the centrifuge that collects vesicles also

37
00:02:23.153 --> 00:02:26.773
brings down protein clumps and other particles of the same size.

38
00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:32.173
Ask whether the activity floats with the vesicles on a density gradient, and whether

39
00:02:32.173 --> 00:02:35.153
it is specific, as the T cell response was.

40
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:36.933
And mind the name.

41
00:02:37.853 --> 00:02:42.073
Vesicles that bud straight from the cell surface look the same, so unless you can

42
00:02:42.073 --> 00:02:45.753
show where a vesicle formed, call it an extracellular vesicle.
